===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[long-standing]] group of people sharing [[cultural]] aspects such as language, dress, norms of behavior and artistic forms.
#: {{ux|en|This '''society''' has been known for centuries for its colorful clothing and tight-knit family structure.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|year=2012|month=March-April|author=John T. Jost|volume=100|issue=2|page=162|magazine=w:American Scientist|title=Social Justice: Is It in Our Nature (and Our Future)?|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/social-justice-is-it-in-our-nature-and-our-future|passage=He draws eclectically on studies of baboons, descriptive anthropological accounts of hunter-gatherer '''societies''' and, in a few cases, the fossil record.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170621102658/http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/social-justice-is-it-in-our-nature-and-our-future|archivedate=21 June 2017}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A group of people who meet from time to time to engage in a common [[interest]]; an [[association]] or [[organization]].
#: {{ux|en|It was then that they decided to found a '''society''' of didgeridoo-playing unicyclists.}}
#* {{RQ:Besant Ivory Gate|III
|passage=At half-past nine on this Saturday evening, the parlour of the Salutation Inn, High Holborn, contained most of its customary visitors.{{...}}In former days every tavern of repute kept such a room for its own select circle, a club, or '''society''', of habitués, who met every evening, for a pipe and a cheerful glass.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1956|month=April|title=Notes and News: Rail Tour of "Old Manchester"|journal=Railway Magazine|page=273|text=The Stephenson Locomotive '''Society''' (North Western Area) and the Manchester Locomotive '''Society''' are co-operating in running a special corridor train, to be worked by an 0-6-0 locomotive of the former Lancashire & Yorkshire Railway, on Saturday, May 12, over a route which includes visits to four historic stations, ascent of the 1 in 27 [[Werneth]] Incline, and traversal of lines normally closed to passenger traffic.}}
## {{lb|en|specifically|UK|Hong Kong}} a primarily student-managed group of members joining together for some common purpose or interest, affiliated with a university's students' union 
##: {{cot|en|club|q1=US, Canada}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} The sum total of all [[voluntary]] [[interrelation]]s between individuals.
#: {{ux|en|The gap between Western and Eastern '''societies''' seems to be narrowing.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-08-10|volume=408|issue=8848|author=Schumpeter
|title=Cronies and capitols
|url=http://www.economist.com/news/business/21583242-businesspeople-have-become-too-influential-government-cronies-and-capitols
|passage=Policing the relationship between government and business in a free '''society''' is difficult. Businesspeople have every right to lobby governments, and civil servants to take jobs in the private sector.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[companionship|Companionship]] or association with one's fellows; friendly or intimate intercourse.
#: {{ux|en|The '''society''' of friends is one of life's great joys.}}
#* {{RQ:Alcott Little Women|chapter=2|passage=This was Jo’s favorite refuge, and here she loved to retire with half a dozen russets and a nice book, to enjoy the quiet and the '''society''' of a pet rat who lived near by and didn’t mind her a particle.{{nb...}}}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The people of one’s country or community taken as a whole. <!-- here's another one ripe for editing -->
#: {{ux|en|Our global '''society''' develops in fits and starts.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=w:Edwin Black|chapter=1|title=Internal Combustion
|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL4103950W
|passage=If successful, Edison and Ford&mdash;in 1914&mdash;would move '''society''' away from the ever more expensive and then universally known killing hazards of gasoline cars:{{nb...}}.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|date=2012-01|author=Steven Sloman|volume=100|issue=1|page=74|magazine=w:American Scientist|title=The Battle Between Intuition and Deliberation|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/the-battle-between-intuition-and-deliberation|passage=Libertarian paternalism is the view that, because the way options are presented to citizens affects what they choose, '''society''' should present options in a way that “nudges” our intuitive selves to make choices that are more consistent with what our more deliberative selves would have chosen if they were in control.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20120108050233/http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/the-battle-between-intuition-and-deliberation|archivedate=8 January 2012}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[high society|High society]].
#: {{ux|en|Smith was first introduced into '''society''' at the Duchess of Grand Fenwick's annual rose garden party.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|passage="What a charming amusement for young people this is, Mr. Darcy! There is nothing like dancing after all. I consider it as one of the first refinements of polished '''society'''."}}
# {{lb|en|countable|legal}} A number of people joined by [[mutual]] [[consent]] to [[deliberate]], determine and act toward a common [[goal]].

